<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gastein Valley</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gasteinertal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gastein Valley is an Alpine valley in the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, south of the Salzach. Its gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines made it a major source of wealth for the prince-archbishops of Salzburg in the 15th and 16th centuries, and its hot springs have drawn visitors since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Bad Gastein became a fashionable spa, where the Gastein Convention was signed in 1865.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gastein-valley.jpg" alt="Gastein Valley" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: / Zeichner: de:User:Triq · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bad_Gastein_Centre_1997-02-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
